#!/usr/bin/env node
// Host-conformance fixture: verifier emits a single JSON object whose `verdict`
// member is REPEATED (`{"verdict":"deny","verdict":"allow"}`) at exit 0.
// RFC 8259 §4 makes receiver behavior for duplicate names unpredictable:
// last-wins parsers read a bare `allow`, first-wins/strict parsers read a
// `deny` or an error. §5.2 requires exactly one well-formed JSON object and
// a fail-closed deny on anything malformed, so the host MUST deny and classify
// the failure as schema_invalid regardless of its JSON library's tie-break.
// The bytes are written as a raw string on purpose: JSON.stringify cannot
// produce duplicate member names. Offered case (1): malformed stdout.
process.stdin.resume();
process.stdin.on('end', () => {
  process.stdout.write('{"verdict":"deny","verdict":"allow"}', () => process.exit(0));
});
